Write the weekly memo for the 2026-10-12 meeting from the single notes file /private/tmp/hirearchy-p1-F1-1793129193/notes/2026-10-12.md (read-only; use no other file). Save it as lead/work/memo-2026-10-12.md. Output: line 1 a one-line title with the meeting date; then one line starting 'Summary:' summarising the meeting in one line; then '## Decisions'; then '## Action items' (markdown table, columns exactly Owner | Action | Due). Nothing else (no attendee list, no recap, no extra sections). Where the notes give no owner/date, write 'none'. Hints from past memos: 'before X' = Due none, keep the wording in Action; a date given for an event is not a deadline for an action unless the action is FOR that event (then Due = the event date, C9); 'We agreed X would do Y' = an action row only; a relative date like 'the day before the freeze starts' becomes an ISO date computed from the notes (C6). Include commitments made in prose (e.g. 'will probably sort it out himself', swaps of shifts) as rows where the notes show a commitment, and decisions stated informally in prose. In your reply, list for each action row the notes sentence it comes from, and flag any item where a criterion could be read two ways.

## Acceptance criteria
C2: At most 400 words as counted by read, table included.
C3: Every decision in the notes file appears under 'Decisions', including decisions stated informally in prose, and no decision that is not in the notes appears.
C4: Every commitment in the notes file appears as one row of the action items table, including commitments made informally in prose (for example 'X said he would ...'), and no row is invented.
C5: The action items are a markdown table with exactly the columns Owner | Action | Due, and each row has exactly one owner, named as in the notes.
C6: Due is an ISO date (YYYY-MM-DD) when the notes give a date or make it clear from the meeting date (for example 'by Friday' becomes the date of that Friday); otherwise Due is 'none', and 'none' is also used when the notes say no date was set.
C7: Every statement in the memo is traceable to the named notes file and to no other file.
C9: When an action is for a dated event, Due is the date of that event (for example, a room booked for the training on the 14th has Due 2026-10-14).
C10: The memo is markdown with a one-line title containing the meeting date, then one line starting with 'Summary:' that summarises the meeting in one line, then a section titled 'Decisions', then a section titled 'Action items', in that order, and nothing else.
(C8: the reviewer's report must show every criterion met, and you apply every required change.)

